    "turn_text": "when I actually started schools, schools, public schools were segregated. And the black school was the best school where my mother attended and my aunt's, Doug was school. And my family was so insistent that we don't participate in any more segregation, even though those were the Black Douglas was the best school, they sent us to Catholic school. And my sister and I were probably the only two Black students for eight years at St. Joseph's Elementary School in El Paso. And it was Sisters of Loretta taught me great education, but it was during that period that You know, I realized that there were a lot of there was a lot of discrimination against us in our community El Paso only had. about 2% African-American population. I couldn't go to the Plaza Theater, couldn't go to the swimming parties. And I had friends at school who invited me to all of these events with them, you know, Latinas, white kids. And so, you know, young people thought it was okay, but I couldn't attend because I was black. And it was at that point, I realized that something went right. And we formed block clubs, girls' clubs around our neighborhood because our neighborhood was black and Latino. We had a couple of blocks of black folks and then Latino communities surrounding. And so it was at that point when I was a young girl in El Paso, Texas, and the only black girl in a lot in the Girl Scout troop, the brownies, you know, at ballet. My mother had all these extracurricular activities for us, but we were always either looked at with suspicious eyes or treated differently. And that formed my kind of views. And finally, I'll just on race and public service, because I knew I had to do something. And finally, because I'll just say when I was born in El Paso in the day, my mother almost died in childbirth because they wouldn't live her into the hospital because she was black. Long story about how she finally",
